#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363479 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172135 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "355c5dfd-d06f-56ef-9c38-8dccf702467e" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6dc64a5-a90d-5dd1-a93d-97925fd04228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3y\\,$ and $x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363481} {:answer/id #uuid "09611345-5534-57f8-ae82-2cede0b9efb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y\\,$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363482} {:answer/id #uuid "1c39a77f-8bbe-502b-85b2-5cb5a5263132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2y\\,$ and $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363483} {:answer/id #uuid "381dc42b-11bf-512a-911f-3e01e2a323be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3{y}^{2}\\,$ and $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363484} {:answer/id #uuid "70992b12-a416-5e5c-aa21-4b0454c3e299" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}^{3}\\,$ and $z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c39a77f-8bbe-502b-85b2-5cb5a5263132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2y\\,$ and $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363483} :answer-field/id #uuid "a753b91d-1a9d-5b6d-831f-daf49b3ab843" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "663a6461-43e7-5235-b081-b1415045b679" :question/math-academy-id "q-162808" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-y+z$ on the intersection of the surfaces ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ and ${x}^{2}+z=4$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨2x,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ &=\\lambda ⟨0,\\,,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {y}^{2}+{z}^{2} &=1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+z &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-y+z\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+z=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨0,\\,2y,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,0,\\,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172135 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "355c5dfd-d06f-56ef-9c38-8dccf702467e" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "062a0d8b-1164-510a-b878-42ee11980575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2\\,$ and $y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493400} {:answer/id #uuid "80a1b39c-24bc-5f2c-84e5-1b4dc7369d3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2x\\,$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493592} {:answer/id #uuid "9013aefb-f1d5-51f5-b261-83ccbece62c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1\\,$ and $x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493619} {:answer/id #uuid "b03b5d8d-43ac-5b85-807e-fd77d3684d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2z\\,$ and $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493674} {:answer/id #uuid "c35373e7-274c-52a1-8823-254d1f74b230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y\\,$ and $-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80a1b39c-24bc-5f2c-84e5-1b4dc7369d3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2x\\,$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493592} :answer-field/id #uuid "a852ffd1-8b11-5f79-833e-b65af8fa8346" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3e1bedf1-a88d-51f4-8239-18dd7d6b26f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162809" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=xy+{z}^{2}$ on the intersection of the surfaces ${z}^{2}+{x}^{2}=4$ and $x+2y=-2$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨y,\\,x,\\,2z⟩ &=\\lambda ⟨\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,0,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨1,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {z}^{2}+{x}^{2} &=4 \\\\ x+2y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+{z}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)={z}^{2}+{x}^{2}=4,\\,h(x,y,z)=x+2y=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=4 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨y,\\,x,\\,2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,\\,0,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨1,\\,2,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172135 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "355c5dfd-d06f-56ef-9c38-8dccf702467e" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1f02490-e174-514c-b237-addfc0f36652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$xy,\\,$ $-y,\\,$ and $2x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363495} {:answer/id #uuid "e1fb72e2-a910-5b6a-b4ad-8e9519cebcfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2y,\\,$ $2x,\\,$ and $2y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363496} {:answer/id #uuid "db6369f2-9d17-5590-b34f-390179ac9dd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2xy,\\,$ $-2x,\\,$ and $4y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363497} {:answer/id #uuid "b52f84e9-49d4-58f3-a677-e6e6618a94b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2x,\\,$ $0,\\,$ and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363498} {:answer/id #uuid "17e269ba-5330-54f6-8386-9e8f93a11f39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2y,\\,$ $2x,\\,$ and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db6369f2-9d17-5590-b34f-390179ac9dd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2xy,\\,$ $-2x,\\,$ and $4y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363497} :answer-field/id #uuid "a31aec0c-583a-5055-888d-4c97b54a776b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a45a55dd-96e0-52af-8d8a-8615ecb90040" :question/math-academy-id "q-162813" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find the maximum of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y+{z}^{2}$ on the intersection of the surfaces $y-{x}^{2}=1$ and ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨\\,,\\,{x}^{2},\\,2z⟩ &=\\lambda ⟨\\,,\\,1,\\,0⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,,\\,2z⟩ \\\\ y-{x}^{2} &=1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y+{z}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=y-{x}^{2}=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2xy,\\,{x}^{2},\\,2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨-2x,\\,1,\\,0⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,4y,\\,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172135 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "355c5dfd-d06f-56ef-9c38-8dccf702467e" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4899d2f0-4a9a-54c0-b255-243f00296fb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x-z,\\,$ $1,\\,$  and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532082} {:answer/id #uuid "7307529a-c5fa-50c3-85e4-0c320e676b56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$yz,\\,$ $1,\\,$  and $z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532192} {:answer/id #uuid "7c3d9025-0e2b-5e52-b2d1-c27a27ac63c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2z,\\,$ $0,\\,$  and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532212} {:answer/id #uuid "900204f4-4ee6-51fa-bded-94c975db4cc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2z,\\,$ $1,\\,$  and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532260} {:answer/id #uuid "d5255563-50f7-52ae-bccd-46caade6840e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$xz,\\,$ $0,\\,$  and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4899d2f0-4a9a-54c0-b255-243f00296fb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x-z,\\,$ $1,\\,$  and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532082} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2718901-61ad-591c-ba8b-c2899ee3b35f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01a5e642-9803-5a1b-8846-85c812cf1513" :question/math-academy-id "q-162815" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find the minimum of the function $f(x,y,z)=xy-zy$ on the intersection of the surfaces $y+z=1$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,-y⟩=\\lambda ⟨0,\\,1,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩ \\\\ y+z=1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy-zy\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=y+z=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨y,\\,x-z,\\,-y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172385 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172134 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "496fa617-6592-57af-89ac-b77d68df0bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "840bf45c-301d-571e-b4b1-2d38f5184997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169174} {:answer/id #uuid "8ce7193e-9c8a-5892-b3b2-9dd5740e5a39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169175} {:answer/id #uuid "0c2c55b4-faed-50a9-9a11-88654eeeea7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169176} {:answer/id #uuid "146ab504-7502-580b-8948-83313a38e7df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169177} {:answer/id #uuid "341f7a97-f751-519e-a656-6d626ffd0b7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169178}] :answer-field/id #uuid "22ec19ad-e286-572c-9400-8d51bcd9c3c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169173} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7cf0d10-0164-5940-b2ff-ac74159c769f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363515} {:answer/id #uuid "2deefb88-233c-55f8-884a-b51ed7f814a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363516} {:answer/id #uuid "f3fa660a-c2c3-51fb-a2d1-4b25af872ac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363517} {:answer/id #uuid "333af397-8729-589a-b6b1-714eee031410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363518} {:answer/id #uuid "1358ff78-9bb2-5bc2-9ce8-ff70c2824014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "333af397-8729-589a-b6b1-714eee031410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363518} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7473673-07d3-5a99-9f25-93a1d422bf85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a33f04b0-b880-5f66-b37e-14b59e173b67" :question/math-academy-id "q-162898" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=x+2y-6z$ on the intersection of the plane $2x-3y+2z=0$ and the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x+2y-6z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=2x-3y+2z=0,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=0 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,2,-6⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2,-3,2⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,2y,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=2\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ 2 &=-3\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ -6 &=2\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{7}{2\\mu } \\\\ y &=-\\frac{7}{2\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{49}{4{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{49}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{49}{2{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{49}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=\\pm 1,\\,y=∓1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $y$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=1$ and $y=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3y+2z & =0 \\\\ 2(1)-3(-1)+2z & =0 \\\\ 5+2z & =0 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-1$ and $y=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3y+2z & =0 \\\\ 2(-1)-3(1)+2z & =0 \\\\ -5+2z & =0 \\\\ z & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(1,-1,-\\frac{5}{2}),\\,(-1,1,\\frac{5}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(1,-1,-\\frac{5}{2})$ | $(-1,1,\\frac{5}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $14$ | $-14$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(1,-1,-\\frac{5}{2}),$ and its maximum value is $14.$"}]}
